BACKGROUND
The European Defence Agency (EDA) was established on 12 July 2004, and is governed by Council Decision (CFSP) 2015/1835 defining the statute, seat and operational rules of the European Defence Agency. The Agency has its headquarters in Brussels.

The main task of EDA is to support the Council and the Member States in their effort to improve the Union's defence capabilities in the field of crisis management and to sustain the Common Security and Defence Policy (CSDP) as it currently stands and as it develops in the future.

The Agency is structured into four directorates. The Corporate Services Directorate (CSD) and three operational directorates: Industry, Synergies and Enablers (ISE); Capability, Armament & Planning (CAP); Research, Technology and Innovation (RTI).

THE AGENCY'S WAY OF WORKING
The Agency is an “outward-facing” organisation, constantly interacting with its shareholders, the participating Member States, as well as with a wide range of stakeholders. It works in an integrated way, with multi-disciplinary teams representing all of the Agency’s functional areas, to realise its objectives. Its business processes are flexible and oriented towards achieving results. Staff at all levels need to demonstrate the corresponding qualities of commitment, flexibility, innovation, and team-working; to work effectively with shareholders and stakeholder groups, formal and informal; and to operate without the need for detailed direction.

THE CORPORATE SERVICES DIRECTORATE
The Corporate Services Directorate (CSD) provides critical business support to EDA operations by delivering high-quality corporate services under seven dedicated organizational elements, namely: Human Resources; Procurement and Contract Management; Finance; IT and Information Management; Security; Legal and Infrastructure. Additionally, the Record Manager is responsible for the effective management of information produced and/or received by the organisation.

CSD processes are geared towards efficient and optimal use of resources, leveraging good practice and technological solutions while ensuring sound financial management, transparency and accountability; outward focus and commitment to continuous improvement are the hallmarks of CSD.

DUTIES
EDA is looking to recruit an experienced procurement, contracting and grant-management professional with strategic vision, sound leadership and management skills, and a proven commitment to developing people, policies and processes. The jobholder will contribute to the delivery of the Agency’s business objectives as part of its dynamic corporate services team.

The jobholder is accountable to the Corporate Services Director and has duties as Head of Procurement and Contract Unit (PCU). This includes:

Core business

- preparing the annual activity plan, in cooperation with the operational Directorates, covering the Agency’s general budget, ad hoc budgets within the meaning of Chapter IV of Council Decision 2015/1835, and assigned revenue, including EU contributions within the meaning of Chapter V of that Decision;
- managing, monitoring and reporting on the implementation of the activity plan, including through appropriate key performance indicators, in close cooperation with the Head of Finance Unit and the relevant staff of the operational Directorates;
- prioritising and steering the procurement and grant-management pipeline, balancing operational urgency, legal and financial constraints, available resources and audit-readiness requirements;
- overseeing the end-to-end preparation, launch, evaluation, award and implementation of ordinary procurement procedures, ensuring compliance with the applicable Financial and Procurement Rules, sound planning, timely delivery and effective support to Authorising Officers by delegation;
- coordinating procurement activities under EDA ad hoc projects and programmes, including category A and B arrangements, by translating participating Member States’ requirements into sound procurement strategies, contractual documents and cooperative implementation arrangements;
- providing guidance and quality assurance for grant and EU-contribution management activities, including calls, award procedures, grant agreements, amendments, reporting and interaction with beneficiaries, while supporting Contracts Administrators and operational services throughout the grant lifecycle;
- ensuring risk-based quality assurance and ex ante control of procurement, contractual and grant-management files, including the anticipation and mitigation of legal, financial, procedural and reputational risks throughout the lifecycle;
- fostering the identification of needs and the development of procurement and grant-management tools and procedures, including guidelines, templates and standard documents;
- proposing strategic developments in the Unit’s portfolio and innovative approaches to management and stakeholder interaction, including with contractors and beneficiaries, in line with EDA strategies;
- promoting continuous business improvement while also integrating recommendations from controlling bodies as well as good practices from comparable EU and international organisations.
- keeping abreast of policy and legislative developments in the EU defence domain, with a view to proactively informing decision-making.

Unit management

- ensuring, through the Senior Procurement Officers responsible for the above three domains, the sound planning and management of the tasks and resources allocated to the Unit;
- ensuring the dynamic reallocation of resources across domains to address peak demands, maintain service continuity and frontload priority activities pending approval of new resources;
- nurturing interdisciplinary approaches across Sectors, including through communities of practice, to strengthen knowledge sharing, methodological consistency and collective problem-solving;
- ensuring business continuity, knowledge retention and succession planning within the Unit, including through the documentation of key processes, the sharing of critical expertise and the progressive development of back-up capacities across Sectors;
- providing guidance, direction and feedback to team members;
- developing the competences and skills of the team in line with evolving needs and performance-management requirements.

Duties may evolve according to the development of EDA’s structure and activities, and the decisions of EDA management.

